// CART decision-tree prediction: flag in-service machines likely to fail,
// learned from the operating history of machines that have already been
// retired with a known outcome. This is the `predict` block made real by the
// predict_decision_tree primitive (issue #71, ADR-0007).
//
// How it reads:
//   - `for records where ...`  — the candidates to score (in-service machines).
//   - `features [...]`         — the numeric axes the tree splits on.
//   - `trained_on records ...` — the labeled history the tree learns from.
//   - `label_attr "..."`       — which attribute on those rows is the outcome.
//   - `confidence >= N`        — drop predictions from an impure leaf (a leaf
//                                whose training rows don't agree strongly).
//   - `{class}` in the label   — the predicted outcome for the row.
//
// A CART tree is trained on the retired machines (Gini-impurity splits, depth
// ≤ 5, ≥ 5 samples per leaf), then each in-service machine is walked down the
// tree to a leaf. The predicted class is the leaf's majority outcome;
// confidence is the leaf's purity. The splits taken *are* the explanation —
// `talon explain` shows "operating_hours > 2000 AND repair_count > 3", not an
// opaque score.
//
//   ./talon build   examples/failure_risk.tln
//   ./talon test    examples/failure_risk.tln test/failure_risk.tln.test
//   ./talon explain examples/failure_risk.tln test/failure_risk.tln.test
//
// Note: predict runs end-to-end under `talon test` / `talon explain`. The
// `talon run` executor path shares the pending training-materialisation work
// tracked for the other supervised/multi-attribute primitives — see ADR-0006.

predict "Failure risk" {
  for records where type == "machine" and status == "in_service"
  features [attr "operating_hours", attr "repair_count"]
  trained_on records where type == "machine" and status == "retired"
  label_attr "outcome"
  confidence >= 0.9
  label "predicted outcome: {class}"
  priority HIGH
}
